WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:04.390
So collective computation is how we think
biological systems solve problems.

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:09.810
But before delving into what collective computation
is, it's worth stepping back and thinking

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.760
about the big picture.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.330
So in physical systems, physics is dominated
by concepts like pressure, entropy and temperature.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.279
These arise from simple collective interactions
and provide huge insight into how the universe

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:24.810
works.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:30.589
In biological systems, there are similar collective
concepts like metabolism, conflict management

00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:31.589
and robustness.

00:00:31.589 --> 00:00:36.870
But unlike in physical systems, these are
functional concepts.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:41.670
Whereas in physical systems order is generated
through the minimization of energy, in biological

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:45.230
systems there is the additional step of information
processing.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:49.210
Why biological systems have this additional
step and whether information processing makes

00:00:49.210 --> 00:00:56.210
them fundamentally subjective and therefore
uncharacterizable by law are big open questions.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:01.570
In C4, my research group here at SFI, we think
that biological systems overcome this intrinsic

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:07.090
subjectivity by computing — in evolutionary,
developmental or learning time — their own

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:13.020
macroscopic worlds, in other words, consolidating
regularities to do work more efficiently.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:19.910
Examples of collective computation include
the coherent behavior that arises at the whole

00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:24.630
organism level from the firing decisions of
the billions of neurons in your brain.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:29.521
It includes the features of social structure
like the distribution of power that arise

00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:34.480
from the status-signaling interactions in
chimpanzee and macaque groups and which have

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.790
huge implications for how conflict is managed.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:43.710
It includes the collective motion of fish
schools that arises from the alignment and

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.470
velocity decisions of individual fish, and
it includes the gene regulatory interactions

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:53.420
in echinoderms that produce robust features
of phenotype.

